WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to&nbsp;
the program today, Stanley McChrystal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:10.400
retired United States Army general and also author&nbsp;
of the very interesting book Risk A User's Guide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.800
I really appreciate your time&nbsp;
today. It's great to have you on.&nbsp;

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.120
Speaker 5: Well, it's my&nbsp;
pleasure, David. Thank you.&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:23.760
Speaker 1: So you talk in the book about how we&nbsp;
all have a sort of immune system to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:33.040
risk that evaluates risk and is is sort of in&nbsp;
an iterative way analyzing what are acceptable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:39.040
and unacceptable levels of risk in life. Can you&nbsp;
talk a little bit about how the system operates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.400
how it's developed, where it comes from?
Speaker 5: Absolutely. As we started studying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.520
for the book, we first came to the conclusion&nbsp;
that the greatest risk to us is in fact us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.040
And we came upon the analogy to the human immune&nbsp;
system and the human immune system is a miracle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:03.600
It basically detects threats that are ingested by&nbsp;
our bodies, analyzes each one for their threat to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.960
us, responds normally, destroys them if necessary,&nbsp;
and then learns from that process. And that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.640
principle behind vaccination, and it does that&nbsp;
about 10000 times a day. They estimate on things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.840
that could make us sick or kill us. And yet we&nbsp;
don't have to spend much time thinking about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:26.080
unless the immune system doesn't work well.&nbsp;
Organizations and actually individuals have what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:33.040
we call a risk immune system, and that's a series&nbsp;
of 10 risk control factors like communication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:43.040
narrative action, timing, leadership, things that&nbsp;
form a holistic ability to respond. And what this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.640
really says is we can't predict every risk or&nbsp;
threat that's going to approach us. We're just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:54.240
not that good at in a complex world. It's a it's&nbsp;
a fool's errand to try. But what we can do is make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.720
ourself more resilient, make our organizations&nbsp;
better able to respond to those things that come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:07.360
at us from every direction and of unexpected form.
Speaker 1: I want to kind of build up to big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.400
organizations like the military and large&nbsp;
corporations, but maybe we could start just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:19.280
with an example at the individual level. I&nbsp;
remember when I was 18, I got a motorcycle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.880
and my parents didn't like it. But I think&nbsp;
they thought if they tell me, get rid of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.480
it'll make me want the motorcycle more. So I think&nbsp;
it was sort of like a let them get it out of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:34.240
system thing. And I had an experience where I hit&nbsp;
a little bump. The front wheel came down and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:40.480
handlebars started getting a little squirrely. And&nbsp;
at that point, I, it was a fraction of a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.640
where I said, this is beyond my risk tolerance. I&nbsp;
realized it's too dangerous and I got rid of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:54.720
Now that one instant decision, I'm guessing, is&nbsp;
coming cumulatively at the end of a whole bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.840
of different inputs over the course of my life, up&nbsp;
to that point that allows me to intuitively assess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.600
that the risk here now exceeds the reward.&nbsp;
How does that system get built up over time?&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.560
Speaker 5: Well, over time, you first learn&nbsp;
the ability to know what's important to you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.000
Typically when you're about 18,&nbsp;
when I had a motorcycle as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.120
I was interested in being cool and going places&nbsp;
and not spending a lot of money to do that. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.800
so that became a very high priority for me, and I&nbsp;
was able to sort of compartment away the idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:30.560
I would set the bike down and get scraped up,&nbsp;
which I did and other things like that. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:36.000
you start with prioritizing what's important.&nbsp;
And then as experience comes, you also learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.400
that things can happen in the military. People&nbsp;
talk about new soldiers being scared in combat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.320
Well, the reality is, every brand new&nbsp;
soldier thinks they're bulletproof&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:53.440
until they realize they are not. So it is older,&nbsp;
more experienced soldiers, in fact, that have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.400
better appreciation for what the dangers of&nbsp;
combat can bring. And they tend to be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:04.160
cautious. They they tend to to do more things,&nbsp;
to prepare effectively, to wear body armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:09.280
to do all those things. So we will learn through&nbsp;
a lifetime. What's important to us, what we've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:15.680
to protect. And then we also learn what are the&nbsp;
things we have to do to put in place protections?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.280
In some cases, it's modify our behavior. In&nbsp;
other cases, it's use different equipment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.520
have a car, not a motorcycle, things like that.
Speaker 1: When it comes to the military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:32.160
it seems that there's this additional component&nbsp;
that that has to fit into the risk calculation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:39.200
which is that elected officials who are outside of&nbsp;
the military make decisions about what they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.880
the military to be doing. So, I mean, thinking&nbsp;
about Iraq as an example, we could we could do an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:52.080
assessment of the kind of risks of that mission&nbsp;
from from a strategic and tactical standpoint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:56.640
that doesn't really seem to account&nbsp;
for the political motivations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.560
exist in even thinking of doing that in the&nbsp;
first place. So how does an organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.800
like the military where risk assessment is so&nbsp;
important? Deal with the fact that much of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.200
is being done is politically oriented?
Speaker 5: Well, you hit a good one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.000
here because they be because&nbsp;
these things are often tension.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.920
You may have policymakers who want to accomplish&nbsp;
something in there. They're prepared to accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:23.440
great risk to the force so they can do that&nbsp;
because they think that the overarching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.800
objective is important. And as long as everybody&nbsp;
is informed and aligned on that, then you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:34.880
operate pretty well. The problem is when you&nbsp;
have them intentions, like in Mogadishu in 1993,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:40.480
there was this mission creep and the force on the&nbsp;
ground thought that their mission was to get Farah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:46.400
headed. They did a number of operations. The risks&nbsp;
grew higher. And then when it ended up on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.760
night of three four October 1993 with the big&nbsp;
firefight, which 18 Americans were killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:58.720
policymakers recoiled because suddenly that&nbsp;
level of loss was a mystic political risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.920
that they weren't prepared to accept. And so&nbsp;
what had happened is you can find people can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:12.480
wander away from being focused and aligned on&nbsp;
the real risks to something and then unexpected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:19.200
outcomes produce really difficult circumstances.
Speaker 1: Did you have situations where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:25.760
were analyzing a situation or a mission&nbsp;
from a risk standpoint in the way that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:31.200
military does it? And then when you present&nbsp;
it to an elected official, you realize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.240
you guys just are not on the same page that&nbsp;
the risk assessment, the way you're doing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:41.680
is disconnected from their political priorities.
Speaker 5: Well, in fact, and often their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.080
political priorities aren't perfectly aligned in&nbsp;
their own minds because they just haven't thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:51.360
through it enough. So, for example, during the&nbsp;
war in Afghanistan, there was a great concern over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:56.240
American casualties, and I absolutely get that&nbsp;
the people that you're serving with, you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:02.720
want them injured or killed at the same time, a&nbsp;
policymaker be maybe saying the important thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:09.760
is to prevent any American casualties. Well, you&nbsp;
can't accomplish the mission if you don't accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:15.680
some level of risk. I go to the loss of Ambassador&nbsp;
Chris Stevens in North Africa a few years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.960
and there was this call that Chris Stevens should&nbsp;
not have been in Benghazi, should not have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:25.600
in a location where he could be at risk. What&nbsp;
I would argue, if you don't want him at risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:31.680
put him in Ohio. The problem is there's a&nbsp;
different risk. He won't accomplish any diplomatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:38.720
objectives. So you've got to understand what&nbsp;
the overall requirement is and then temper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.880
your risks according to that. And too often&nbsp;
we have policymakers, people on the ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:47.520
in different players not aligned with a&nbsp;
common understanding of what the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.160
mission is and what the risks involved are.
Speaker 1: How could those things be better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:57.600
aligned? Because it seems fundamental to the way&nbsp;
that the military operates, which is the military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:03.840
operates in one way, whereas their missions are&nbsp;
often guided by different considerations and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:07.040
creates this conflict that you're talking&nbsp;
about or this in. Congruence, you may be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:11.840
Is there some way that it could be organized&nbsp;
differently so that those are better aligned?&nbsp;

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:17.440
Speaker 5: Well, first is communication. And of&nbsp;
course, a clear narrative provided by leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:21.760
and the military is not clean on this. It's not&nbsp;
like the military's got it right and other people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:26.480
don't. Sure. If you give military people&nbsp;
a requirement, often they will say, Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:32.960
the the thing that is least risky to us is to&nbsp;
use massive fire support, pummeling the area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:37.440
go in and occupy, and that reduces the risk&nbsp;
to our soldiers that we will accomplish the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:44.880
overall mission. But if in doing so, you alienate&nbsp;
the population or you create popular resentment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:50.080
around the world to what you do, then what you've&nbsp;
done is you've actually lost the big objective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:56.400
By trying to reduce risks at the local level&nbsp;
in Afghanistan, we had a period going into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:02.160
an area in Helmand where the local commander used&nbsp;
a term called courageous restraint. We are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:07.840
to have our soldiers operate in a way that is&nbsp;
actually riskier for them, their personal safety.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:12.640
But it's more aligned with what we have to&nbsp;
do to win the support of the Afghan people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:18.480
And that's where this communication has to occur&nbsp;
because policymakers can see soldiers at accepting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.920
risk and they go, No, I don't want them to accept&nbsp;
risk when in reality you got to be focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:28.800
what's going to produce the most important&nbsp;
outcome, the overall mission that you're therefore&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:35.200
Speaker 1: in in the midst of these missions. You&nbsp;
know, sometimes we hear that not that it's easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:41.360
but that it's typical that individual service&nbsp;
members will say, you know, I'm not here because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.400
I decided that this is the mission we should&nbsp;
be doing. I'm here, I'm a cog in the wheel or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.880
I'm doing my part and there's, you know, a&nbsp;
chain of command, et cetera. And the political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:56.080
considerations are not important. But then in&nbsp;
the later years of the Iraq War in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:01.600
as I remember it vividly, we started to hear more&nbsp;
stories from people coming back and saying when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:07.360
we were there, we were talking about if this was&nbsp;
their opinion, why are we even here? Iraq didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:12.400
do 911. I don't understand what we're doing, and&nbsp;
that started to become a factor for some. Not for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:16.560
all. Obviously, many, many believe that that we&nbsp;
should have been there. Can you talk a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:23.680
bit about how common that is and how it impacts&nbsp;
service members to be on a mission that they may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:29.280
think is not one that should be even on the table?
Speaker 5: I think it's very common in almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:34.720
every war war, and it comes partly from a&nbsp;
misalignment on what the actual mission is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:39.120
And there's also comes when the frustration&nbsp;
if you don't think you're accomplishing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:45.040
many people don't understand that. In 1864,&nbsp;
President Abraham Lincoln thought that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:48.880
going to lose reelection and he thought&nbsp;
he was going to lose reelection because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:54.080
people in the North had soured on the war and&nbsp;
particularly in the eastern theater. They had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:59.760
not seen a tremendous amount of success and&nbsp;
so fresh frustration soldiers were saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:07.440
I am fighting in a war that we are not winning and&nbsp;
therefore I'm not accomplishing anything. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:13.680
that happened in Afghanistan, that happened in&nbsp;
Iraq. You also see things that you know with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:20.160
Iraqi partners or Afghans. You see corruption or&nbsp;
a lack of effectiveness, and you become cynical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:24.160
You go, Why am I here doing my best and&nbsp;
risking my life when in fact, the people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:30.800
we are theoretically helping aren't doing an&nbsp;
equal amount? That's that's Age-Old. You'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:36.560
never do away with that. Hmm. You do try&nbsp;
to address it so that soldiers understand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:42.160
But then you also try to make sure soldiers&nbsp;
understand the overall purpose of their mission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:48.880
It's really important for commanders and political&nbsp;
leaders to constantly explain to soldiers, why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:55.840
are they doing this? If I think of soldiers in the&nbsp;
Philippines in 19 early 1942, as the Japanese were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:01.200
moving to conquer it, which resulted in the&nbsp;
Bataan Death March, many of them felt that those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:05.440
they'd been abandoned and there was no point in&nbsp;
trying to defend something that was indefensible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:11.280
And it gets down to communication and leadership
Speaker 1: when it came to Afghanistan, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:17.840
this is an area that you know well and the recent&nbsp;
withdrawal that President Joe Biden carried out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:26.160
My perspective was when Donald Trump proposed&nbsp;
it. I liked the idea. And when Joe Biden did it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:30.800
I liked the idea. And my belief is that&nbsp;
the problems that took place would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:35.680
taken place no matter when it was done and that&nbsp;
fundamentally, as you talk about the big picture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:40.960
versus the nitty gritty, it was the right thing&nbsp;
to do. Do you have thoughts about how it went,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:45.440
given that you spent so much time there?
Speaker 5: Yeah, I'm probably more forgiving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:50.880
on the actual problems at the end, and some&nbsp;
people might be, and the first reason is when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:57.840
President Trump did the Doha Accords and agreed&nbsp;
that Americans would leave by one May 2021. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:02.000
was ending the forever war, and that was what the&nbsp;
consensus of the American people really wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:06.960
have happen. Yes. And so the next 18 months,&nbsp;
the Taliban didn't attack Americans because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:13.440
it wasn't in their interest. They just wanted us&nbsp;
to leave. The danger was from Al Qaeda and ISIS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:18.880
who had a reason to want to attack us on the&nbsp;
way out. So as this small American force, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:25.840
down about 2500 people when it came time to reduce&nbsp;
that force and begin the actual withdrawal. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:31.600
going to have increasing risk as it gets smaller&nbsp;
because less ability to defend yourself. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:34.800
of course, that the question is why can't&nbsp;
the government? Why couldn't the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:41.200
of Afghanistan maintain legitimacy and its&nbsp;
military maintain coherence during that process?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:47.840
Well, what I would say is the Afghan government&nbsp;
struggled with legitimacy from the beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:52.960
Partly, but their own fault and partly for&nbsp;
factors, but also the Taliban did a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:58.960
good information warfare campaign so that right&nbsp;
after the Doha Accords were signed for 18 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:03.680
they went around the country and they said as soon&nbsp;
as the Americans leave, we're going to flip power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:11.120
here. And so no, no rational Afghan official&nbsp;
or military wants to be the last person killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:17.680
in a losing cause. So the Taliban didn't conquer&nbsp;
Afghanistan, they occupied it. You know, when when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:23.280
the air went out of the balloon, I know that we&nbsp;
we say it's an intelligence failure by American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:27.600
intelligence, community or military not&nbsp;
to know that what's going to happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:34.880
And I would say this is more art and&nbsp;
science. It was hard. So I don't think it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:39.760
went like we wanted it to. I don't think&nbsp;
the overall outcome certainly doesn't make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:45.920
anybody happy. But but I don't think it was the&nbsp;
fiasco that is sometimes described. There were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:49.200
risks involved and people knew that.
Speaker 1: Do you think it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:52.880
have been just as chaotic had Donald&nbsp;
Trump stuck to his original timeline?&nbsp;

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:58.320
Speaker 5: I think the risk of it would have would&nbsp;
have been there, I don't think that would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:02.480
changed the risk of that. You can't guarantee&nbsp;
it would have happened, but I don't see any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:06.240
reason to believe it would have been different.
Speaker 1: Fair, fair. I guess we should talk a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:11.040
little bit about organizations and corporations&nbsp;
when we talk about risk. Just to go back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:15.120
start of our conversation, we've talked about&nbsp;
individuals in the military. It seems one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:22.160
the unique things about the idea of a corporation&nbsp;
is that the corporation is a legal entity. It's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:29.440
idea, but it's made up of individuals. And because&nbsp;
of the size of some very large corporations, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:35.040
well as a kind of imbalanced risk reward for the&nbsp;
individuals where they could make a decision that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:40.080
has the potential to make them a lot of money, but&nbsp;
has relatively little downside because at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:45.840
of the day, there is this apparatus called an S&nbsp;
corporation or a C corporation that that exists&nbsp;&nbsp;

